## Proof

Let $A = \{x \in [0,1] : \text{the decimal expansion of } x \text{ contains infinitely many 7s}\}$.

We compute $\lambda(A)$ where $\lambda$ is Lebesgue measure.

**Step 1: Reduce to the complement.**

Let $B = [0,1] \setminus A = \{x \in [0,1] : \text{the decimal expansion of } x \text{ contains only finitely many 7s}\}$.

We will show $\lambda(B) = 0$, which gives $\lambda(A) = \lambda([0,1]) - \lambda(B) = 1$.

**Step 2: Decompose $B$ as a countable union.**

For each $n \geq 0$, define
$$B_n = \{x \in [0,1] : \text{no digit in position } n+1, n+2, n+3, \ldots \text{ of } x \text{ is a 7}\}.$$

If $x \in B$, then $x$ has only finitely many 7s, so there exists some $n$ such that all 7s occur among the first $n$ digits. Hence $x \in B_n$. Conversely, $B_n \subseteq B$ for every $n$. Therefore
$$B = \bigcup_{n=0}^{\infty} B_n.$$

**Step 3: Show $\lambda(B_n) = 0$ for each $n$.**

Fix $n \geq 0$. For each $k \geq 1$, let $E_k = \{x \in [0,1] : \text{the } k\text{-th digit of } x \text{ is not 7}\}$. Under Lebesgue measure on $[0,1]$, the digit in position $k$ is uniformly distributed on $\{0,1,\ldots,9\}$, so $\lambda(E_k) = \tfrac{9}{10}$.

The digits in distinct positions are independent under Lebesgue measure (this follows from the fact that Lebesgue measure on $[0,1]$ is the product measure of uniform measure on $\{0,\ldots,9\}$ under the digit map, up to a countable exceptional set of measure zero). Therefore, for any finite set of positions $S$,
$$\lambda\!\left(\bigcap_{k \in S} E_k\right) = \left(\tfrac{9}{10}\right)^{|S|}.$$

Now $B_n = \bigcap_{k=n+1}^{\infty} E_k$. For any $N > n$,
$$B_n \subseteq \bigcap_{k=n+1}^{N} E_k,$$
so
$$\lambda(B_n) \leq \lambda\!\left(\bigcap_{k=n+1}^{N} E_k\right) = \left(\tfrac{9}{10}\right)^{N-n}.$$

Since $\left(\tfrac{9}{10}\right)^{N-n} \to 0$ as $N \to \infty$, we conclude
$$\lambda(B_n) = 0.$$

**Step 4: Conclude.**

Since $B = \bigcup_{n=0}^{\infty} B_n$ is a countable union of measure-zero sets,
$$\lambda(B) \leq \sum_{n=0}^{\infty} \lambda(B_n) = 0.$$

**Remark on decimal expansion ambiguity.** Numbers with two decimal expansions (terminating vs. trailing-9s) are exactly the rationals of the form $k/10^m$, a countable set of measure zero. This exceptional set does not affect the measure computation above.

Therefore
$$\lambda(A) = \lambda([0,1]) - \lambda(B) = 1 - 0 = 1.$$

The Lebesgue measure of $A$ is $\boxed{1}$.

